The change window is still open. Before this goes to the customer, one more
pass: look for optimizations you have NOT yet considered. Go back to the
measured memory of this cluster (the dataflows and operators that are still
large after your changes, the ones you left alone, the ones you never
opened) and look for further levers of any kind: index or boundary changes,
hint changes, query rewrites, drops, anything that reduces this cluster's
memory while keeping the results of every materialized view exactly
unchanged and every materialized view a materialized view.

Do not stop at ideas. For each new optimization you find, validate it on
this bench: build the candidate, prove exactness in both directions, measure
before and after, and apply it if it clears the same gate as before (low
risk, or an estimated saving of at least __GATE__ MB). Where a candidate
fails validation, say so and keep the measurement. Where it is a big
structural rewrite of the SQL, deliver the design, the estimate and the
invariant it depends on, and STOP, as before.

Your report must have exactly these sections:
1. NEW FINDINGS: each new optimization, how you found it, and what it is
   worth (estimated MB saved, with confidence).
2. VALIDATION: per finding, what you built, the exactness proof, the
   before/after measurement, and the outcome (applied, rejected, or design
   only).
3. CHANGES: the exact SQL you applied this round, in the order you applied
   it.
4. UPDATED CUSTOMER DOC: the consolidated change document updated for this
   round (self-contained, supersession explicit).
